<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gobelins Manufactory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1662">1662</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gobelins Manufactory is a tapestry works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, on the site of the dyeing workshops of the Gobelin family. In 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> bought it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and made it a royal manufactory producing tapestries and furniture for the royal palaces under the painter Charles Le Brun. It became a model of state-sponsored luxury industry and still operates under the French state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gobelins-manufactory.jpg" alt="Gobelins Manufactory" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manufacture_des_Gobelins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Gobelins were a family of dyers who settled in the middle of the 15th century in the Faubourg Saint-Marcel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, on the banks of the Bièvre. In 1602 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> rented space from them for his Flemish tapestry makers, Marc de Comans and François de la Planche. Their sons Charles de Comans and Raphaël de la Planche took over the workshops in 1629, but their partnership ended around 1650 and the works were split in two. Tapestries from this early Flemish period are sometimes called pre-gobelins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the purchase of 1662 the works became a general upholstery factory, where tapestries and furniture of every kind were made under Charles Le Brun, director and chief designer from 1663 to 1690. Workshops there also served as foundries for most of the bronze statues in the park of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. Louis XIV's financial problems closed the establishment in 1694, but it reopened in 1697 to weave tapestry, chiefly for royal use, and rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a> works until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> suspended its work.</p><p>The factory was revived under the Bourbon Restoration, and in 1826 the making of carpets was added to that of tapestry. In 1871 the building was partly burned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, and Jean-Camille Formigé built a new one on the avenue des Gobelins in 1912. A branch was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, probably early in the 18th century, in what is now Fulham High Street; around 1753 the priest and adventurer Pierre Parisot appears to have taken it over, but it closed a few years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The manufactory stands at 42 avenue des Gobelins in the 13th arrondissement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and is run by the administration of the Mobilier National and the national carpet and tapestry manufactories of the French Ministry of Culture. Its four irregular 17th-century buildings hold Le Brun's residence and looms that still weave tapestries by 17th-century techniques, now in limited numbers and with contemporary subjects for French government buildings. The Galerie des Gobelins, built in the gardens by Auguste Perret in 1937, holds temporary exhibitions and reopened on 12 May 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gobelins Manufactory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Jean-Baptiste Colbert the crown promoted royal manufactures such as the <b>Gobelins</b> tapestry works and backed projects such as the Canal du Midi, finished in 1681.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
